---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifric/ifric6.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Question IFRIC 6 Answers" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Two Candidate Obligating Events" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Conclusion – Market Participation" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Measuring the Liability" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Why This Reasoning Generalises Beyond WEEE" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Interaction with New vs Historical Equipment" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRIC 6 vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A pure "obligating event" question</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 37 recognises a provision only where a PRESENT obligation exists as a result of a PAST event. IFRIC 6 is entirely about pinning down what that past event actually is, in a fact pattern where two plausible candidates exist – and the answer determines whether a liability is recognised years earlier or is instead spread out over future periods.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Faithful representation over front-loaded caution</div>
        <div class="principle">Recognising the full liability at the moment historical equipment was manufactured – before any current obligation to fund its disposal actually exists – would overstate liabilities relative to the entity's true present legal position. IFRIC 6's conclusion ties recognition to the event that ACTUALLY creates the obligation: ongoing participation in the market during the relevant measurement period.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRIC 6 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 6 addresses the accounting for the obligation to participate in the cost of waste management for "historical" household equipment – i.e. products sold before a specified cut-off date – under waste-management schemes modelled on the EU's Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment (WEEE) Directive.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL FACT PATTERN</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>A producer sold household electronics before a legislative cut-off date establishing collective producer responsibility</span>
          <span>The producer continues to sell into the same market after the cut-off date</span>
          <span>National law allocates historical waste management costs based on producers' market share during a defined future measurement period</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Historical waste</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Household equipment put on the market before the specified cut-off date, whose waste management costs are funded collectively by producers who participate in the market after that date.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Measurement period</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The defined future period during which market share is measured for the purpose of allocating historical waste management costs among current market participants.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Question IFRIC 6 Answers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A deceptively simple timing question</div>
        <div class="principle">Should a producer recognise its share of historical waste management costs (a) when the historical household equipment was ORIGINALLY manufactured or sold, or (b) progressively, as the producer PARTICIPATES in the market during the relevant future measurement period? The two answers produce dramatically different recognition timing and liability size at any given reporting date.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Two Candidate Obligating Events</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Weighing the alternatives</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-18"><span>Candidate obligating event</span><span>Why it was considered, and why rejected</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-18"><span>Manufacture or sale of the historical equipment</span><span>REJECTED because, at that date, no law yet existed creating a present obligation on the producer specifically; the future law's cost-allocation mechanism does not attach to the individual units sold</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-18"><span>Participation in the market during the measurement period</span><span>ACCEPTED – under most such schemes, a producer's allocated share of historical waste costs is calculated by reference to its market share DURING the measurement period</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Conclusion – Market Participation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The obligating event</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 6 concludes the obligating event is PARTICIPATION IN THE MARKET during the measurement period, NOT the manufacture or sale of the historical equipment. A liability is therefore recognised progressively as the entity participates in the market.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Worked example – progressive liability build-up</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Market-share-based allocation</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A national scheme allocates $10,000,000 of historical waste management costs among producers based on market share during the calendar year. By 30 June, a producer holds a 15% market share for the year to date and expects to hold approximately 15% for the full year.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">A provision of approximately $1,500,000 (15% × $10,000,000) is built up progressively over the measurement period as the entity participates in the market – NOT recognised in full at the start of the year.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Measuring the Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Estimation mechanics</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Because the final allocation depends on FULL-PERIOD market share, which is only known with certainty at the end of the measurement period, entities must ESTIMATE their expected market share as the period progresses and true up the provision accordingly – consistent with IAS 37's general measurement principles.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Why This Reasoning Generalises Beyond WEEE</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">A template for "levy-style" obligations generally</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IFRIC 6's core reasoning – that the obligating event for a cost allocated based on a future activity measure is PARTICIPATION IN that activity, not some earlier triggering event – has proven influential well beyond the specific WEEE fact pattern.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Interaction with New vs Historical Equipment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">A narrower scope than it first appears</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IFRIC 6 addresses ONLY the treatment of historical (pre-cut-off) equipment costs. Waste management obligations relating to NEW equipment sold after the cut-off date are typically the direct, individual responsibility of the producer that sold those units, and are accounted for under general IAS 37 principles.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What IFRIC 6 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Nature of the waste management scheme and the basis for cost allocation (e.g. market share)</span>
          <span>Basis for estimating the provision, including the estimation of expected market share for the measurement period</span>
          <span>Uncertainties affecting the timing or amount of the obligation</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Identifying the true obligating event under the specific terms of a national or regional waste-management scheme</span>
              <span>Distinguishing historical equipment costs (in scope) from new equipment costs (outside IFRIC 6's specific reasoning)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Expected market share for the full measurement period, estimated progressively as actual data becomes available</span>
              <span>Total scheme-wide historical waste management cost pool to be allocated</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRIC 6 vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IFRIC 6 vs US GAAP (ASC 450)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRIC 6 (IFRS)</span><span>US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Dedicated interpretation for market-share-based levies</span><span>Yes – IFRIC 6 specifically</span><span>No direct codified counterpart; analysed under general ASC 450 loss contingency principles</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Obligating event analysis</span><span>Explicit – market participation during the measurement period</span><span>Determined case-by-case, generally reaching a similar conclusion</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Because the underlying regulatory schemes themselves differ meaningfully between the EU and US, this comparison is more about ANALYTICAL APPROACH than about a head-to-head technical rule difference.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRIC 6 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Recognition of the provision as market participation occurs</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Built up progressively over the measurement period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>True-up of estimated market share</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Change in accounting estimate under IAS 8</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Settlement of the allocated cost at period end</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Cash outflow against the recognised provision</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Supplies the core recognition, measurement, and disclosure framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs subsequent revisions to the estimated market-share allocation</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRIC 21</span><span>A later, broader interpretation on levies echoing IFRIC 6's obligating-event reasoning</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Obligating event</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The past event that creates a present legal or constructive obligation, leaving an entity no realistic alternative to settling it – the central concept IFRIC 6 works through for this fact pattern.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Cut-off date</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The date separating "historical" equipment (funded collectively based on market participation) from "new" equipment (funded directly by the individual producer).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Obligating event", definition: "The past event that creates a present legal or constructive obligation, leaving an entity no realistic alternative to settling it." },
      { term: "Cut-off date", definition: "The date separating 'historical' equipment from 'new' equipment for waste management cost allocation purposes." },
      { term: "Collective producer responsibility", definition: "A regulatory model under which current market participants jointly fund waste management costs for products sold before a specified date." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRIC6} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>




